Turning a raw URI into a safe navigation action means resolving it against your app's actual route table and validating both structure and authorization before navigating — the same discipline covered in mobile security's deep-link handling, applied here to the routing layer specifically.

```kotlin
fun resolveDeepLink(uri: Uri): NavTarget? {
    return when {
        uri.pathMatches("/order/{id}") ->
            NavTarget.OrderDetail(id = uri.pathSegment(1), requiresAuth = true)
        uri.pathMatches("/product/{id}") ->
            NavTarget.ProductDetail(id = uri.pathSegment(1), requiresAuth = false)
        else -> null  // unknown route — fall back to home, never crash on an unrecognized path
    }
}
```

The navigation layer then has to decide what to do if `requiresAuth` is true and the user isn't logged in: the common pattern is queue the target, show login, and resume navigation to the original target on success — rather than either discarding the link (bad UX) or navigating straight into an authenticated screen with no session (a crash or a broken screen).
